trade-offs that we see now

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.110
and going forward.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:48.600
So a bit of background--

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:49.860
what is Segment?

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.388
What's the scale that
we're seeing today?

00:02:52.388 --> 00:02:54.180
For those of you who
might not be familiar,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.120
Segment gives you a
single API to collect data

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.010
about your users.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.220
These might be things like users
loading pages on your website.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.890
Maybe it's signing up
via your mobile app.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.920
Maybe it's completing orders
on your online checkout.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.230
Whatever it is, your users are
doing things with your app,

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.180
and you want to
know who they are

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.440
and why they're doing the
things that they're doing.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.630
Concretely, what
that looks like is

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.610
that Segment gives
you the set of sources

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.140
that you see on the left.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.000
These are places
where your data lives,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.330
whether it's your mobile app,
website, or maybe a source

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.310
like Stripe or Zendesk.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.220
We will take that data
into one consistent place

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.650
and then get it into hundreds
of different SaaS tools

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.110
that you might be using
over on the right.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.630
These could be data warehouses
like Redshift or BigQuery.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.650
Maybe it's an email tool
like Customer.io or Zendesk.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.560
Or maybe it's an analytics
tool like Google Analytics

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.190
or Mixpanel.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.430
Whatever it is, Segment helps
you get that single source

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.720
of truth about
your data into all

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.610
of those different destinations.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.220
Now by the numbers,
this has taken us

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.310
to some interesting places.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:08.287
Today we collect data for
19,000 users every single month.

00:04:08.287 --> 00:04:09.870
And in terms of our
intake, we process

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.750
about 300 billion events
every single month, as well.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.120
That translates to
roughly 450 billion

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.149
outbound API calls that
we're making, and processing

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:21.860
terabytes of data per day.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.740
Now to date, all
this infrastructure

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.110
has run on Amazon.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.248
It's running in Amazon's
compute environment,

00:04:28.248 --> 00:04:30.040
and we're processing
all of that data using

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.680
ECS and a fleet of
16,000 containers.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.160
And under the hood, it
looks something a little bit

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.620
like this.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:40.630
We have our API
at the front door,

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.050
which is ingesting events.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.830
It's then publishing
those to Kafka,

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:47.170
which we use as our
primary message bus,

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.150
where we're collecting all
these different sorts of events.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.980
And then from there
we have a consumer

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.710
who's reading each message,
reading from a database,

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.410
and figuring out where
that data should be sent,

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:01.020
and then deciding to send that
data to certain destinations.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.560
Maybe this event is bound
for Google Analytics.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.840
This other one is
built for Salesforce.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.145
Another one is even
sent to Mixpanel.

00:05:08.145 --> 00:05:09.770
And maybe we have
one which is supposed

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.593
to go to all of the above.

00:05:12.593 --> 00:05:14.010
Now there's a
really big advantage

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.100
of this system in the way
that we've architected it.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.260
And that's that it's stateless.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.290
If we want to add more
capacity to the system,

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.510
it gives us more throughput.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:27.420
We can simply just double the
amount of collection points,

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:30.710
the amount of Kafka brokers,
the amount of consumers.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.015
And really the only shared
thing here is our database.

00:05:33.015 --> 00:05:34.890
But maybe we can get
past that with some read

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.560
replicas and some caching.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.680
In our case, it's really easy
to scale out more capacity

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.030
as our load doubles,
triples, 10x's or 50x's.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.770
It kind of doesn't matter.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.230
But in 2018, our product
team started giving us

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.088
a new set of requirements.

00:05:52.088 --> 00:05:54.380
In particular, it was to
launch this new product called

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.850
Personas.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:58.070
The idea was a take
Segment from this place

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.710
which simply streamed
individual events around,

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.690
where each event
maybe doesn't have

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:05.360
any correlation to the
others, to actually creating

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.420
the idea of a user profile,
something that's a little more

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:13.512
holistic who tells you what the
user is and what they're doing.

00:06:13.512 --> 00:06:15.220
Hopefully it should
tell you a little bit

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.160
about who they are,
where they've been,

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.320
and allow you to query
that in real time.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:24.730
As you might guess, this use
case is decidedly stateful.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:28.450
We can't any longer just add
nodes to our infrastructure

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.700
and expect them all to
fan out and kind of work

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.570
in this magical way.

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:35.740
Instead, now we actually have to
pull our data together and put

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.200
it into one spot, so
that if we query it

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:42.565
we get a both consistent
and available answer.

00:06:42.565 --> 00:06:44.690
To give you a better sense
of what these staple use

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:47.540
cases look like, let me walk
you through the three use

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.220
cases of Personas.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:53.538
The first one is what
we call the profile API.

00:06:53.538 --> 00:06:55.080
The general idea
here is that we have

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.480
some set of information
about the user, who they are,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.680
what they've done.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.740
And we want to be able
to pull that information

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.840
programmatically.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.110
Now there's two use cases
which we found this for.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:09.380
The biggest one is for building
personalization systems

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.130
in your web app.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.090
Let's say you're
running an online store.

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:15.060
You sell all different
types of pants--

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.615
men's pants, women's
pants, kid's pants.

00:07:17.615 --> 00:07:20.670
But you've seen a user come
back in the past 10 times

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:24.910
in the last month, and they've
only looked at men's pants.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.880
Maybe you should change
up your home page.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.460
Make it guide them a little
bit towards finding only men's

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.100
pants, so they don't have to
click around and potentially

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.340
abandon your entire
checkout flow.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:39.140
The second use case,
after the profile

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:43.675
API for personalization, is
around identity resolution.

00:07:43.675 --> 00:07:45.300
As I said before,
we're collecting data

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.010
from many different places.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:48.330
Maybe it's your web app.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.950
Maybe it's your mobile app.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:53.040
Maybe it's another source
like Zendesk or Stripe.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.860
Each of those users may
get a different identity.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.620
Maybe it's their email.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:01.890
Maybe it's the ID that you
have in your user database.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:05.460
Or maybe it's something else,
like a Stripe customer ID.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.100
We want to be able to take
those different individual

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.100
identifiers and unify
them, so that if you

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.860
want to query for a
user it doesn't matter

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:19.290
if they're Calvin at Segment or
if they're XYZ123, or Stripe,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:22.170
some other CUS_whatever.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.330
We want to be able to
say, here's Calvin.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.986
Here's my user.

00:08:26.986 --> 00:08:29.860
And finally, the third use
case after the profile API

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:33.370
and identity resolution
is audience computation.

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.659
Now for those of you who
might not be familiar--

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:38.100
I certainly wasn't when
we first started Segment.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.789
An audience is just
a group of people

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:42.260
who meet certain criteria.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:45.600
You want to be able to
specify certain rules to say,

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.410
hey, which of my users
have visited three times

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.680
in the past month, started
a checkout flow last week,

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.060
but then never completed it?

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.670
Personas gives you this ability
to create those arbitrary

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:01.290
rules, and then find
that list of users.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:02.460
So that's Personas.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:05.670
You query users in real
time via the profile API,

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.220
match them by identity,
and then create

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:09.720
audiences of users, as well.

00:09:11.765 --> 00:09:14.140
Let's take a look at how it's
architected under the hood.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.360
Now I wanted to start
by first talking

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.100
about lambda architectures.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:25.062
And don't worry, I'm at
the right conference.

00:09:25.062 --> 00:09:27.020
This is a different type
of lambda architecture

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:30.080
than the serverless one
you might be hearing about.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.330
Now this lambda
architecture actually comes

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:35.600
from this guy, Nathan
Marz, who is at Twitter.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.580
And he kept coming up
with these concepts

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:42.158
of how he could process lots
and lots of data in real time.

00:09:42.158 --> 00:09:43.700
And he wanted to
ensure that he could

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.220
crunch both large amounts
of data, potentially

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.440
terabytes or
petabytes, but also get

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.940
a good real-time view of
individual parts of analysis

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.590
that he is running.

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.180
And so he came up with the
system that you see behind me.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:58.530
The general idea is
that new data comes in

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:02.800
via single source, but
then it's written twice.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.300
First, it's written
to a batch layer,

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.450
some sort of job that's
running maybe hourly or daily,

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:11.690
is doing big roll ups and
aggregations of a bunch

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:14.100
of different pieces of data.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:18.420
From there, he also writes data
to what he calls a speed layer.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.670
You could think of this as
being something like Kafka,

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.587
or the queue that we've
been keeping track of.

00:10:22.587 --> 00:10:25.960
This effectively has the diff
of whatever the batch layer has.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.380
Then, at query time,
you effectively

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.690
want to pull those
two pieces together.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.060
You can grab your big
aggregation that you have,

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:34.890
as well as your little
real-time updates

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:35.890
that you've been making.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:42.320
Now if you look at this, we have
a few different use cases here.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:43.700
We have batch computations.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.370
We have speed to get us the
best of both worlds in terms

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:49.610
of aggregation and in
terms of real-time updates.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.770
It actually doesn't look
that different from what

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:53.630
we want on Personas.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:57.590
We want to be able to query
our profiles in real time

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.360
and match users by identity.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.630
And we want those actions to
happen incredibly quickly,

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:04.790
ideally under a second.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:07.600
But we also want to
create audiences of users.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.190
And this is where we
scan terabytes of data.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:13.820
And maybe it's OK if it
takes a few minutes or maybe

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:16.600
even an hour to come back.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:18.310
Now you might be
thinking, well, it

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:19.977
sounds like you have
different pipelines

00:11:19.977 --> 00:11:21.580
with different requirements.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.770
Why not use different
data stores?

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:26.990
And that's exactly what we did.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.680
We effectively have our
single message bus in Kafka,

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.360
then publishing
to Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:35.170
and from there we have two
separate sets of workers

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:38.260
who are writing the data twice.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:40.930
One set of workers is writing
that data into BigQuery

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:44.640
to do batch processing,
to create those audiences.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.520
The second set of workers
are writing into Bigtable

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:52.117
to give us real-time
results and updates.

00:11:52.117 --> 00:11:54.450
I'm going to start on the
left hand side of this diagram

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:56.910
before moving into the
right to really explore

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:59.600
some of these use cases.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.650
Now for those of you
who aren't familiar,

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:05.020
Kafka and Pub/Sub both are this
kind of idea of a message bus.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:07.090
Each one can take
individual messages

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:09.880
and then replicate
them for delivery.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.590
To give you a better sense of
what Segment messages actually

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:17.660
look like, I've included an
example over here on the right.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:19.370
Segment messages
involve tracking things

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:22.460
like page views,
user events, maybe

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:24.920
it's completed order
events, whatever it is.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:27.580
Here, I just took an
example from my blog.

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:30.580
Typically these are
semi-structured JSON.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:34.600
So Segment, as part
of our API spec,

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:37.600
we specify that there are a few
top level fields that you need.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:41.710
These will be things like the
user ID, the event name, maybe

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:44.800
a timestamp to let us know
when the event happened.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.110
But the rest is all filled
in by our customers,

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:49.185
depending on what's
important to them.

00:12:49.185 --> 00:12:51.560
So, for example, here can see
that there are a few custom

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:52.700
properties.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:54.080
It's the name of the article.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:55.402
Maybe it's the referrer.

00:12:55.402 --> 00:12:57.110
If you're running a
music app, maybe it's

00:12:57.110 --> 00:13:00.170
something like the song
title or the artist.

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:04.470
Whatever it is, we can't really
anticipate it ahead of time.

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:07.530
It's just our users
sending it into our API.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.480
So that's a key part of our
data processing pipeline.

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:13.670
And typically, these
messages run to about 1

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:16.210
kilobyte in terms of average,
though they may span up

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:19.120
to 32 kilobytes depending
on how much stuff is just

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:21.493
packed into that message.

00:13:21.493 --> 00:13:24.160
To give you a sense of what this
actually looks like from a code

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:27.610
perspective, I have included
basically a light version

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.860
of the code that
takes data from Kafka

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.930
and publishes it into Pub/Sub.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:35.020
Effectively, we iterate over
a range of messages here.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.750
We create a payload of
them, tagging each message

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:40.840
with its type and collection,
basically what sort of thing

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:44.760
it is, whether it's a user,
product, or an account.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:48.700
We pack that all into JSON, and
then we compress it via Snappy.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:50.200
And then we ship
it off the Pub/Sub.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.920
And from there we're
trying to analyze

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:59.880
these hundreds of thousands
of 1 kilobyte messages, where

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.950
for one branch we
send it into our batch

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:04.800
commutations in BigQuery,
and for the second branch

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.020
we send into the real-time
computations in Bigtable.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:14.550
So let's dive into
each of those, BigQuery

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:16.497
and Cloud Bigtable.

00:14:16.497 --> 00:14:18.330
For each one, I'd like
to compare them first

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.360
on the use case that
we're trying to achieve.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.650
Second, the underlying
architecture

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:25.400
pulled, from the best
of my understanding,

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:28.130
from white papers and papers
that are published externally

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:29.780
by Google.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.420
Third, we'll talk about their
data model, what it actually

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:36.050
looks like to store Segment
data in these systems.

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:38.690
And then fourth, we'll
discuss the query patterns,

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:42.048
how we're actually using
this in production.

00:14:42.048 --> 00:14:43.340
So let's revisit this use case.

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:47.610
On the top here,
we have the worker,

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.280
which is running and
loading data into BigQuery.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:52.460
And because this is a
batch job we can run it,

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:55.780
let's say, every 15 minutes
or kind of whatever schedule

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.067
we want.

00:14:57.067 --> 00:14:58.900
Separately, we're running
and then computing

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:01.450
this data via the
compute service, which

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.570
is effectively just running a
cron job to calculate audiences

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:08.410
for our users every
so many minutes.

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:09.910
And again, we want
to find users who

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:12.360
meet arbitrary criteria here.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.940
We don't know what those
criteria are ahead of time.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.560
It's up to our users
to specify them.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:19.760
We have terabytes of data
sitting in the system,

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.270
and we need to get responses
within a few minutes.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:23.920
But it doesn't
have to be seconds,

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:26.650
milliseconds, nanoseconds.

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:30.730
We rarely care about all of the
columns, which is pretty key.

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.440
Oftentimes, we'll be
only trying to say, hey,

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.940
who are users who have done
event X more than Y times,

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:40.750
or maybe find the only
users in San Francisco.

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:44.050
In this case, we're picking one
tiny part of our information

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:47.290
and then extracting
a list of user IDs.

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:49.860
And again, real-time reads
are not a big deal here.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.620
We need maybe only tens
of concurrent queries

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:54.420
to satisfy our user base.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:58.970
And so as long as we're able
to get that, we're good.

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:03.100
So let's look at what BigQuery
looks like under the hood.

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:06.338
Now I'm going to do a
quick history lesson here.

00:16:06.338 --> 00:16:07.880
Internally at Google,
there have been

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:09.590
a number of systems
for crunching

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:11.310
large amounts of data.

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:14.090
And back in 2004, kind of
the seminal paper on this

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:15.780
was called MapReduce.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:19.310
And at the time, Google was
trying to compute basically

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:22.460
the entire index of the web.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.700
Sounds like a small goal, right?

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:26.540
And so with MapReduce
they said, hey,

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:29.020
we want to build a
crawl the entire web.

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:31.510
Then we want to be able to
figure out page rank for each

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:32.620
of these pages.

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:35.590
We want to be able to say which
pages are those popular based

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:38.740
upon which pages
then link to them.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.110
And so MapReduce was
this internal system

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:43.360
that Google had where
as the programmer,

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:45.300
you needed to write
two functions.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:48.612
You need to write a map
function and a reduce function.

00:16:48.612 --> 00:16:51.270
And as long as you
specified those two things

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:52.920
and then passed
in your data set,

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:54.990
you'll be able to
get your results back

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:58.572
within maybe hours or
maybe the next day.

00:16:58.572 --> 00:17:00.030
But it was the best
thing that they

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:03.983
had for literally web scale data
at the time, so they used it.

00:17:03.983 --> 00:17:06.150
And they were able to parse
a lot of free form text.

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:13.790
But then in 2010 a new paper
came out called Dremel.

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:16.310
And this is really the
basis for BigQuery.

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:18.310
Now in the paper they say
that this has actually

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:21.130
been put around since 2006.

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:23.829
But Dremel was designed to solve
a different set of problems

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:25.569
than MapReduce.

00:17:25.569 --> 00:17:30.030
In particular, it was designed
for interactive analysis.

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:32.720
Now you can think
of Dremel as sort

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:34.470
of being the internal
version of BigQuery.

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:35.928
But in terms of
how they're set up,

00:17:35.928 --> 00:17:38.070
my understanding is that
they're roughly the same.

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:42.150
For BigQuery and Dremel, that
interactive piece is key.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:43.740
Now, instead of
being an analyst who

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:46.200
had to wait hours
or maybe overnight

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:48.450
to get back your
results, you just

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.260
get them within a few minutes.

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:52.050
So if you were
exploring your data,

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:53.967
it could actually give
you a really good sense

00:17:53.967 --> 00:17:55.650
of what was in it.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:58.170
The other big advantage
of Dremel and BigQuery

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:02.160
was that it used SQL
rather than programming.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:05.970
Before, you might end up writing
a big MapReduce job saying,

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:07.560
yes, this looks great.

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:09.920
And then you ship it off
to whatever your MapReduce,

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.362
or maybe externally a
Hadoop cluster looks like.

00:18:12.362 --> 00:18:14.070
And then when you come
back, it turns out

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:16.290
some part of your
program had errored,

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:19.370
which means that you have to
run the entire thing again.

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:22.310
Because BigQuery
and Dremel use SQL,

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:26.090
it meant that it was accessible
to your average analyst

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:30.680
and could get you back data as
well as errors in a very, very

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.750
quick timescale.

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:34.800
Additionally, it uses
nested structured data,

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:36.890
which I'll talk about
a little bit more here,

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:39.363
rather than the free form
text that MapReduce just

00:18:39.363 --> 00:18:41.780
says, OK, you have some sort
of input of bytes and output.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:47.710
In particular, I'd like to
highlight four good ideas that

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:50.440
are all pulled from the
Dremel and BigQuery paper.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.240
And these are all
ideas that we've

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:55.960
used to successfully
scan terabytes

00:18:55.960 --> 00:19:00.120
of data within a few minutes.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.775
The first idea is
being column-oriented.

00:19:02.775 --> 00:19:04.650
And let me talk a more
about what this means.

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:10.220
Suppose we want to
build a database.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:13.790
We want to create something
where I can add data to it.

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:15.200
I can query it.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:18.780
And I know that that write data
is somehow written durably.

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:21.090
How might we do it?

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:24.600
Well, let's assume we're doing
the dead simple thing here.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.520
And I'm just adding
to a flat file,

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:29.970
where each row involves
a new row in my database,

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:31.640
or a new record.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:35.220
Maybe first I have the name
of the engineer on my team.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:37.230
Maybe I have their job position.

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:39.060
Maybe I have how
many contributions

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:40.710
they've had in the last year.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:43.176
And then maybe I
have their location.

00:19:43.176 --> 00:19:45.138
And on the face of
this, this kind of looks

00:19:45.138 --> 00:19:46.180
like a standard database.

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:46.790
Right?

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:48.140
It's like, if I want more rows.

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:49.720
I can add them to the end.

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:51.970
If I update, I could
update in place

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:54.820
or I could potentially add
to the end and merge later.

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:59.220
It seems like it does what
I want, just as a flat file.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.780
But now let's get back
to our problem statement.

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:04.750
What if my database
has billions of rows

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:07.760
and I only need one of them?

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:10.557
What if I only need the
location data, for example?

00:20:10.557 --> 00:20:12.640
Remember, we want to be
able to scan all this data

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:15.310
incredibly quickly.

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:18.320
Well, the good idea
that BigQuery had is,

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:21.740
hey, let's store
columns, not rows.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:23.990
If we look back at my super
simple database example

00:20:23.990 --> 00:20:26.930
here, instead of
storing a single file

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:29.420
with each row
indicating a record,

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:31.350
let's instead split them up.

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:34.690
Let's keep one file
for each column.

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:38.410
Then if I want to grab the
location itself, it's easy.

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:40.272
I just read the location file.

00:20:40.272 --> 00:20:41.980
And now I don't have
to parse through all

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:44.272
of this unnecessary data that
maybe I don't care about,

00:20:44.272 --> 00:20:47.230
for things like the
person's name or their role

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:49.175
or how many contributions
they've had.

00:20:49.175 --> 00:20:50.800
And I don't have to
split those commas,

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:52.630
do all that CSV processing.

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:55.465
Instead, I just get what I want.

00:20:55.465 --> 00:20:57.840
And under the hood, it turns
out that BigQuery and Dremel

00:20:57.840 --> 00:20:59.580
do the same exact thing.

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:02.790
Instead of storing rows as
records or records as rows,

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:07.210
rather, they keep columns which
individual nodes can store.

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:08.990
So if you only need
to grab one thing,

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:12.020
you just scan super quickly and
then you get your result back.

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:16.450
The second good idea
is actually related

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:22.160
to being column-oriented,
and that's using compression.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:23.660
As you might have
noticed, if I have

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:26.030
all of these columns on disk--

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:29.240
San Francisco, San Francisco,
Tokyo, San Francisco,

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:30.880
San Francisco--

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:33.170
we have a lot of
repeated data here.

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:36.160
And in particular, if I need
to scan all that data on disk

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:39.310
or if it's from a
backing data file storer,

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:41.770
I need to do a lot of
network transfer costs,

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:45.960
as well as just raw
seeking of the data itself.

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:49.490
So instead we can compress
it by using something

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:52.033
like run-length encoding.

00:21:52.033 --> 00:21:53.700
The general idea here
is that, let's say

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.410
I have 200 entries
which all have

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:57.570
San Francisco as the entry.

00:21:57.570 --> 00:22:01.020
I can now encode them
and say San Francisco 200

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:04.350
before reading the next
column, which maybe is Tokyo 1.

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:07.720
And then maybe I have
another San Francisco 100.

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:11.180
In practice, when I have a
really, really big data set,

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:14.100
this makes scanning it and
parsing it much, much quicker.

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.900
So we have column-oriented
and compression.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:21.610
The third idea that
I'd like to touch on

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:23.830
is an efficient nested encoding.

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:27.725
And I'll get back to why this
is important in a few minutes.

00:22:27.725 --> 00:22:29.350
But to give you a
sense of what exactly

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:33.620
this is, BigQuery gives
you the ability to specify

00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:35.080
what your data looks like.

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:37.430
Here it's represented
as protobufs,

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:41.540
which is a technology that we
also use internally at Segment.

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:43.820
Instead of storing
all of that data

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:47.490
and serialized it as JSON
or some other format,

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:51.350
BigQuery actually keeps
this idea of a field column

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:53.060
and a depth column,
which will let

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:55.760
you know if you're parsing
that data whether you're still

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:58.960
in the same field or not.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:00.930
And honestly, this
goes into more depth

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:03.600
than I'm able to share
during this quick talk.

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:05.310
But to give you
an example, here's

00:23:05.310 --> 00:23:08.002
what happens when you
select all of your fields.

00:23:08.002 --> 00:23:11.500
BigQuery will actually generate
a finite state machine,

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:14.900
which figures out depending
on what that record field

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:19.340
and depth field are, how it
should traverse your document.

00:23:19.340 --> 00:23:21.820
And then from there,
it actually pulls apart

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:23.950
that finite state
machine to parse it

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.690
into its own structs or
protobufs, or whatever it is.

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:30.510
This makes sure that if you're
parsing billions of records

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:32.910
you can do it extremely
quickly, without having

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:34.800
a lot of overhead of
trying to figure out

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.810
sizing and allocations
for those results.

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:42.770
So we had a bunch of
tactics for making

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:47.260
scanning on an individual
machine much quicker.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:50.170
The last one I'd like to share
is this idea of adding servers

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:52.360
to increase efficiency.

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:55.060
Obviously, if we're querying
these terabyte data sets

00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:57.640
on Google Cloud hardware,
it's much different

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:00.040
than if those data sets are
just running on my laptop

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:03.310
within my own little database.

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:06.060
Now BigQuery makes
use of this same idea.

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:08.650
Effectively if you're a
client, you take data--

00:24:08.650 --> 00:24:12.040
or first you hit the root server
and you submit your query.

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:14.740
And then there's a set of
intermediate servers, which

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:16.510
are basically figuring
out how to fan out

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:20.200
that query to individual leaf
nodes, where each of those leaf

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:23.620
nodes then does only a
small subset of the work

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:26.650
and passes back the results.

00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:28.810
To get an example of
what this looks like,

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:30.670
let's say you're
querying the root node.

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:33.670
And maybe for this example
you have just one single layer

00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:35.940
in our tree.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:39.010
If we get our responses
back, that root node actually

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:42.790
has to merge all of our
data, which could potentially

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:44.860
be a lot.

00:24:44.860 --> 00:24:48.020
Instead, as is
discussed in the paper

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:51.700
and as this technique that's
used behind the hood, what

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:54.370
ends up happening is there are
different layers, which each

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:56.750
individually split up the work.

00:24:56.750 --> 00:24:58.820
So now if we issue
a query, there

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:01.380
are a bunch of intermediate
mergers that happen.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:06.240
So each node has to deal with
much, much smaller data set.

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:08.710
The great thing here is that
more servers effectively

00:25:08.710 --> 00:25:10.505
means more distributed work.

00:25:10.505 --> 00:25:12.880
And the fact that it's running
in this giant multi-tenant

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:15.637
system that's been in place at
Google for the past 10 years

00:25:15.637 --> 00:25:18.220
means that we have a pretty good
confidence that those servers

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:21.250
are doing a lot of
shared computation

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:24.910
and getting good utilization.

00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:27.180
So those are BigQuery's
four good ideas--

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:29.910
being column oriented,
compressing data

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:32.100
so it's read quickly
from a single node,

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:35.130
using a fast nested data
encoding, which also supports

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:38.790
arrays and nested objects,
and distributing the work,

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:40.652
especially by separating
data and compute.

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:46.740
Let's look at how that data
model now maps into Personas,

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:51.220
and how nuts to bolts
we're using BigQuery.

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:53.740
Remember, as I said before,
we want to allow users

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:55.810
to create custom audiences.

00:25:55.810 --> 00:26:00.520
These are just groups of users
who meet certain criteria.

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:02.950
We effectively want
to take this criteria

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:05.710
and turn it into,
more or less, a SQL

00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:08.200
query with custom parameters.

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:10.990
But how we do this could
be difficult. Right?

00:26:10.990 --> 00:26:13.280
It's like we have this
kind of a custom UI.

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:14.873
And if we add new
features to it,

00:26:14.873 --> 00:26:17.290
we don't want to then have to
be building a bunch of stuff

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:20.520
on the back end to translate it.

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:23.810
So what we do is we convert
it to an intermediate format.

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:27.920
We convert it into JSON as part
of an abstract syntax tree.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:33.720
And then from there we actually
convert that AST into raw SQL.

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:36.900
It's all machine
generated, but to our user

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:38.520
it gives them this
nice interface

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:41.550
that meets them on their
level, while, for us, it

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:43.410
gives us the power
and flexibility of SQL

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:46.098
on the back end.

00:26:46.098 --> 00:26:48.140
In terms of how we're
actually storing this data,

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:52.220
we keep a data set per customer
to provide isolation and better

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:53.920
monitoring across them.

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:57.780
And then we keep a table per
each collection and event.

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:00.080
Remember, a collection is
just the type of thing,

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:02.840
whether it's a user,
account, or product.

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:05.595
Additionally, we keep tables for
things like traits, identity,

00:27:05.595 --> 00:27:08.200
and merging.

00:27:08.200 --> 00:27:10.200
And in particular, I want
to call your attention

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:12.930
to this set of external IDs.

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:17.025
This is the nested, repeated
format that BigQuery allows.

00:27:17.025 --> 00:27:19.890
And it's one that you won't
find in a lot of other data

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:22.740
warehouses or big data stores.

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:26.620
The fact that this allows us to
specify multiple external IDs,

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:29.190
whether it's an email or
whether it's a database ID

00:27:29.190 --> 00:27:31.380
or whether it's a
Stripe ID, means

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:35.390
that if we want to query for
any one of those, we can.

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:38.430
And we can be sure that it's
quickly findable and indexed,

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:41.580
and we're not spending a
bunch of time parsing JSON

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:44.020
or trying to dig
into these objects.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:47.700
It's right there as
a top level field.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:49.760
Additionally, we
end up exploding

00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:52.790
all of these properties
that the user passes in.

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:56.030
In our V1, we actually stored
these specifically as JSON,

00:27:56.030 --> 00:27:57.458
and kind of in its own field.

00:27:57.458 --> 00:27:59.000
But what we found
is that we actually

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:02.210
got much better performance
by exploding them out, doing

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:05.930
the alterations of the table
when we're uploading that data,

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:07.970
and making sure that
we're able to query it

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:09.421
quickly and efficiently.

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:13.320
So let's look at
the query patterns.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:16.280
How is this being
used in production?

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:18.880
Remember, this is the job
of the compute service.

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:22.100
The compute service is sending
data, or sending queries

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:24.560
across a handful at a time.

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:27.040
And it's trying to run
those as an aggregate,

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:28.580
as sort of a cron job.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:30.490
Maybe every few minutes
it's saying, OK,

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:32.930
what's new with this
audience for customer A?

00:28:32.930 --> 00:28:35.060
What's new with this
audience for customer B?

00:28:35.060 --> 00:28:37.938
What's new with this
audience for customer C?

00:28:37.938 --> 00:28:39.480
What this looks like
under the hood--

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:42.620
and I grabbed a few queries
from our production account--

00:28:42.620 --> 00:28:45.300
is that this is creating
these tens of queries

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:48.360
per second, which honestly
looks like garbage.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:51.082
And it's because it's
all machine generated.

00:28:51.082 --> 00:28:53.040
At the end of the day,
we don't care about what

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:54.402
these queries look like.

00:28:54.402 --> 00:28:55.860
We only care that
our customers are

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:57.943
able to have flexibility
when it comes to querying

00:28:57.943 --> 00:29:00.056
those different data sets.

00:29:00.056 --> 00:29:01.920
And in terms of
actual query times,

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:03.840
we see good
performance, as well.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:06.960
We're able to query gigabytes
or terabytes in a matter

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:08.010
of seconds or minutes.

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:13.580
Additionally, this
gives our users

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:16.463
the ability to preview
particular users who

00:29:16.463 --> 00:29:18.380
might fall into this
audience, because they're

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:20.090
able to interactively query.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:22.850
So they can check and see,
hey, did the audience I built

00:29:22.850 --> 00:29:24.700
actually work out properly.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:27.200
And we also give them insights
about potentially overlapping

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:31.550
audiences that they might have,
areas where user IDs might also

00:29:31.550 --> 00:29:34.060
fall into this audience.

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.560
Looking at our [? numbers ?]
in production by today,

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:39.630
we're currently scanning about
2 gigabytes every single second,

00:29:39.630 --> 00:29:42.160
which corresponds to
about 170 terabytes a day.

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:43.880
And we're using about 800 slots.

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.513
So that roughly wraps
up our BigQuery section.

00:29:49.513 --> 00:29:50.930
We run our batch
computations here

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:52.850
for tens of concurrent queries.

00:29:52.850 --> 00:29:54.270
We're partitioning
it by customer,

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:57.710
so we get good visibility
and isolation across them.

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.660
And we end up keeping
these repeated

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:02.030
arrays of external
IDs, so that if we

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:04.610
want to query by a
particular ID, we can.

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:11.320
Let's shift gears to the speed
side of the equation now.

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:15.970
Remember, we want to be able
to query for this profile API.

00:30:15.970 --> 00:30:20.320
In this case, we can't get data
that's minutes or hours old.

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:21.015
Right?

00:30:21.015 --> 00:30:23.230
But we also don't care
about querying for data

00:30:23.230 --> 00:30:25.210
across all of our users.

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:29.440
Instead, we want to get data in
real time for the specific user

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:32.090
when they're on the web page.

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:36.940
And that takes this bottom
path, the speed layer.

00:30:36.940 --> 00:30:39.770
The use cases here
are much different.

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:42.340
Instead of having to crunch
our gigabytes or terabytes

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:44.860
worth of data across
all users, now we're

00:30:44.860 --> 00:30:48.610
simply looking at kilobytes
to megabytes of data.

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.190
We want to primarily
index it based upon user,

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:53.860
and then maybe to a
second extent, time.

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:56.885
And we need a high
read and write rate.

00:30:56.885 --> 00:30:59.510
We're potentially doing tens of
thousands of queries per second

00:30:59.510 --> 00:31:01.020
here.

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:05.120
And again, data should be
reflected in real time.

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:11.350
We want this to happen in
under 300 to 500 milliseconds.

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:13.980
Now, it's interesting when I
was reading the Bigtable paper,

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:17.142
that this is actually
not that new of an idea.

00:31:17.142 --> 00:31:18.600
It turns out Google
Analytics keeps

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:21.990
their raw [? Quick ?] Table
in the same sort of format.

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:25.650
And so in terms of building
out Bigtable for ourselves,

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:28.630
we figured, hey, this
actually makes a lot of sense.

00:31:28.630 --> 00:31:32.137
We just have to figure out how
to make it work with Segment.

00:31:32.137 --> 00:31:34.470
And to give you an idea, let's
now dig into the Bigtable

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:37.250
architecture.

00:31:37.250 --> 00:31:40.580
So Bigtable, the paper,
was released in 2006.

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:42.410
And it talks about a
distributed storage

00:31:42.410 --> 00:31:44.552
system for structured data.

00:31:44.552 --> 00:31:46.010
Effectively, you
can think about it

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:48.600
as kind of a big key
value store, where all

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:50.212
the keys themselves are sorted.

00:31:50.212 --> 00:31:51.920
So if you want to get
little bits of data

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:56.260
or maybe a range of keys,
you're able to do it.

00:31:56.260 --> 00:31:59.300
Under the hood it looks
something like this.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:01.870
We typically have a client,
which is making queries

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:04.553
to individual Bigtable nodes.

00:32:04.553 --> 00:32:06.970
Each of those Bigtable nodes
are keeping an in-memory data

00:32:06.970 --> 00:32:09.030
structure that they
call a mem table, what

00:32:09.030 --> 00:32:11.260
you think of as a sort of
list of keys and values

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:15.100
that are append only,
that then it also backs

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:18.220
on associated Google
file system tablets.

00:32:18.220 --> 00:32:19.810
These are the
storage layer where

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:22.390
we're sure that we're
persisting the data to make sure

00:32:22.390 --> 00:32:25.870
that in case there's some sort
of crash or an [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:25.870 --> 00:32:28.330
away, that that data
is still replicated

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:31.220
and is still durable.

00:32:31.220 --> 00:32:33.582
Here's what happens
when we issue a write.

00:32:33.582 --> 00:32:35.790
First, that write gets
directed to the right Bigtable

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:37.248
node via some sort
of master lookup

00:32:37.248 --> 00:32:41.220
table, kind of like Chubby,
or another lock service.

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:43.620
And then it actually gets
appended to the in-memory data

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:47.760
structure on the node itself,
before being backed or appended

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:50.900
to the backing storage, as well.

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:53.652
What this means is that
writes are really fast.

00:32:53.652 --> 00:32:55.360
They end up being
these fast appends that

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:57.670
don't require a lot
of locking, and you

00:32:57.670 --> 00:32:59.650
can write extremely quickly.

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:02.590
But then they're mirrored
by the underlying storage

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.100
infrastructure.

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:07.470
Reads, similarly
can also be fast.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:10.090
Let's say we want
to read a new key.

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:12.520
If it's in our in-memory
data structure, which

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:15.610
we can filter for based
upon using a Blum filter,

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:18.750
we can probably still detect
that it's there and then run

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:23.870
a look up in memory to get a
result that's really quick.

00:33:23.870 --> 00:33:26.690
If that's the case, then we pass
back the value from that node

00:33:26.690 --> 00:33:28.670
and we get a very
quick read, especially

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.200
if the locality is good.

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:31.850
If we've written
the value recently,

00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:35.040
then chances are it's
probably still in memory.

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:38.610
If not, we actually fetch that
offset from the backing storage

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:39.603
hardware.

00:33:39.603 --> 00:33:40.770
And then we can get it back.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:47.790
Now in this case, reads
first cache and then

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:49.450
they get merged together.

00:33:49.450 --> 00:33:53.490
So our write path is super
fast, but our read path

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:54.673
is pretty quick, too.

00:33:54.673 --> 00:33:57.090
Because it's coming from memory
most of the time, and then

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:00.670
it's just merging whatever
the diffs are together.

00:34:00.670 --> 00:34:02.410
You might be
thinking, though, what

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:04.970
about if there's an
underlying failure.

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:07.640
What if one of these
nodes goes down?

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:10.170
Well, in that case,
we're OK, too.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:12.570
Let's say we have the
same read path, which

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:15.120
is fetching data from
our backing storage,

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:17.389
and this node dies.

00:34:17.389 --> 00:34:19.580
Well, because we've
separated compute

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:21.800
from the underlying
storage, we're

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:25.820
able now just to move those
portions onto a new Bigtable

00:34:25.820 --> 00:34:28.790
node, and claim that
it now has ownership.

00:34:28.790 --> 00:34:31.100
And now we're able to just
read that same data, which

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:35.938
goes to the same underlying
storage, and get it back.

00:34:35.938 --> 00:34:38.230
And those are the things that
make Bigtable work really

00:34:38.230 --> 00:34:39.730
well for our use case.

00:34:39.730 --> 00:34:41.139
It's multi-tenant.

00:34:41.139 --> 00:34:42.639
It's row-oriented.

00:34:42.639 --> 00:34:44.560
It uses a log
structure merge tree

00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:47.659
under the hood to ensure that
there's fast reads and writes.

00:34:47.659 --> 00:34:50.427
It has immutable
in-memory caching.

00:34:50.427 --> 00:34:51.969
And it does a lot
of different tricks

00:34:51.969 --> 00:34:53.860
to make sure that in
individual node level

00:34:53.860 --> 00:34:58.360
it's saving us both
on reads and writes.

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:01.330
Now let's talk about how
it comes to our data model,

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:04.582
how Segment fits into here.

00:35:04.582 --> 00:35:06.040
Remember I said
before that this is

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:09.710
used to query data
about individual users.

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:15.450
So we actually keep separate
data tables for each data type.

00:35:15.450 --> 00:35:18.830
We have one for records,
letting us know who the user is.

00:35:18.830 --> 00:35:21.350
We have one for
properties, giving us

00:35:21.350 --> 00:35:23.320
various traits about them.

00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:25.190
And we have one for
events, actually storing

00:35:25.190 --> 00:35:27.440
raw event payloads if we
want to pass those back

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:28.990
to the front end.

00:35:28.990 --> 00:35:31.310
The keys are
typically ID and time

00:35:31.310 --> 00:35:33.650
ordered so that we're able
to get a range, let's say,

00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:36.220
give me the events that happened
in the past five minutes.

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:38.140
And the values are
Snappy encoded,

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:40.285
so that if we want
to pull them we can

00:35:40.285 --> 00:35:43.510
do so quickly and efficiently.

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:46.090
And in particular when
we stitch these together,

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:48.280
we keep different
tables for different

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:49.940
read and write profiles.

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:52.298
So we'll actually
use different ones,

00:35:52.298 --> 00:35:54.340
depending on what sort of
query patterns we have.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:59.820
I'd like to wrap up by sharing
a little bit about how this

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:01.591
has gone for us in production.

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:06.160
In production today
with Cloud Bigtable,

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:10.030
we're currently writing
55,000 rows per second,

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:13.720
and reading a little
north of 3x that.

00:36:13.720 --> 00:36:15.820
We're storing 10 terabytes
of data in the system.

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:18.350
And it's all being powered
by 16 different nodes.

00:36:18.350 --> 00:36:20.350
You can see here that
we're describing all of it

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:21.370
within Terraform.

00:36:21.370 --> 00:36:23.250
So if we need to
make a change, we

00:36:23.250 --> 00:36:25.370
can do so quickly and easily.

00:36:25.370 --> 00:36:28.740
For BigQuery, we're running
hundreds of queries per minute.

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:31.150
This is a lot less than
the Bigtable throughput,

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.460
because it's not
designed to be real time.

00:36:33.460 --> 00:36:35.110
It's only for a
small subset of users

00:36:35.110 --> 00:36:37.120
creating aggregate
pieces of data

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:39.800
on top of their
individual user profiles.

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:43.410
Here we're scanning
gigabytes of data per minute.

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:47.990
And we're storing 500 terabytes
worth of data, overall.

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:51.820
But let's get back
to that hard choice.

00:36:51.820 --> 00:36:54.290
If we're choosing
between different clouds,

00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:57.680
I think BigQuery is just
straight up hard to compare.

00:36:57.680 --> 00:36:59.870
There aren't many
other clouds out there

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:03.520
which offer the same sort
of multi-tenant, heavily

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:07.570
battle-tested infrastructure
that BigQuery does.

00:37:07.570 --> 00:37:10.180
It's able to give you
large amounts of data

00:37:10.180 --> 00:37:14.800
querying in incredibly
quick query times.

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:17.550
And to my knowledge, there isn't
anything out there, with maybe

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:19.690
of the possible
exception of Snowflake,

00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:21.490
that even comes
close when it comes

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:24.700
to actually using this data.

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:27.490
But we will look at Bigtable,
there are actually a few more

00:37:27.490 --> 00:37:30.290
alternatives out there,
which seem to exist.

00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:31.930
You could use Dynamo.

00:37:31.930 --> 00:37:33.860
You could host your own
sort of foundation DV

00:37:33.860 --> 00:37:36.047
setup, kind of whatever it is.

00:37:36.047 --> 00:37:38.380
There are a few different
places where Bigtable actually

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:41.540
really shines for us.

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:44.380
Now when we first started,
we ended up using Dynamo.

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:47.420
And we kept running into
problems that made it really,

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:49.960
really hard to work with,
particularly around scaling,

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:52.930
cost, and identifying hotkeys.

00:37:52.930 --> 00:37:56.120
And for those of you
who are familiar,

00:37:56.120 --> 00:37:59.870
remember each Bigtable node
hosts some subset of keys.

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:03.580
So if we have one user who
is recording a lot of data

00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:05.860
all at once or one
particular key that

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:09.460
is sending hundreds of
thousands or tens of thousands

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:13.850
of writes per second, that
individual key gets really hot.

00:38:13.850 --> 00:38:17.380
It ends up being accessed
far more than the rest.

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:19.043
And finding those
keys can be tricky.

00:38:19.043 --> 00:38:20.460
Because typically
what will happen

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:22.668
is they'll bring down the
rest of your infrastructure

00:38:22.668 --> 00:38:25.710
and just slow everything
down, and it's incredibly hard

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:28.542
to figure out what happened.

00:38:28.542 --> 00:38:30.500
So when we're using Dynamo
we ended up actually

00:38:30.500 --> 00:38:32.360
admitting metrics
around these hotkeys,

00:38:32.360 --> 00:38:34.340
and then coming up with
a system to figure out

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:36.720
where this was happening.

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:38.622
But in Bigtable they
just gave it to us.

00:38:38.622 --> 00:38:40.080
There's an out of
the box tool that

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.900
tells you what you're read
and write pattern looks like.

00:38:42.900 --> 00:38:45.210
It tells you how the keys
that are being accessed.

00:38:45.210 --> 00:38:48.510
And you can just go in and
click and figure out which

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:50.970
users are hurting you most.

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:53.820
And this system is honestly
invaluable for debugging,

00:38:53.820 --> 00:38:55.200
because it
immediately highlights

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:57.970
where the problems are.

00:38:57.970 --> 00:39:00.105
The second one is
mostly around cost.

00:39:00.105 --> 00:39:03.283
At Segment, we have a
very write-heavy workload.

00:39:03.283 --> 00:39:05.200
Most of the time we just
have all these events

00:39:05.200 --> 00:39:08.170
that are coming in, and it's
honestly a little bit rarer

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:09.970
that we have users
who are querying

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:13.410
that data via our APIs.

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:15.540
As it turns out,
Bigtable is much better

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:19.770
at splitting this data
between compute and storage.

00:39:19.770 --> 00:39:22.320
And what's more, unlike
some of the other solutions

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:24.237
out there where you may
be charged differently

00:39:24.237 --> 00:39:26.340
for writes and
reads, for Bigtable

00:39:26.340 --> 00:39:28.330
your charged the exact same.

00:39:28.330 --> 00:39:30.800
You're just charged for a node.

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:35.300
And so if we want to be writing
more data than we're reading,

00:39:35.300 --> 00:39:38.210
the cost profile actually
benefits us significantly,

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:41.240
because we're able to separate
that compute and storage

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:43.820
and just be charged for
the raw throughput, rather

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:46.977
than anything special about
our custom workload profile.

00:39:49.600 --> 00:39:54.720
So in closing, I wanted to
leave you with a few thoughts.

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:56.400
This is Segment
Personas, powered

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:58.950
by Bigtable and BigQuery.

00:39:58.950 --> 00:40:02.370
Bigtable helps us for
small random reads.

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:05.550
BigQuery is what we use
for the batch operations.

00:40:05.550 --> 00:40:07.740
We're able to process
billions of events.

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:10.470
And we use a large,
multitenant architecture,

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:14.160
so that each customer's data
gets their own individual

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:16.410
tables and data
sets, but that we're

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:20.210
able to run the same compute
infrastructure everywhere.

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:23.685
This infrastructure gives us
favorable read and write costs,

00:40:23.685 --> 00:40:25.310
and already we've
been able to generate

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:29.650
millions of dollars in
revenue from this new product.

00:40:29.650 --> 00:40:32.290
Additionally, we have
the firm knowledge

00:40:32.290 --> 00:40:35.146
that as we continue
to scale and grow,

00:40:35.146 --> 00:40:39.000
many of these systems that we
have underlying will change.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:43.390
But we know that under the
hood, both BigQuery and Bigtable

00:40:43.390 --> 00:40:47.095
are able to scale out to levels
far beyond what we see today.

00:40:47.095 --> 00:40:48.470
So if you're
considering building

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:50.540
your own infrastructure
upon them,

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:52.670
I'd highly recommend
investing in Google

00:40:52.670 --> 00:40:55.910
for your different data stores.

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:56.990
Thank you.

00:40:56.990 --> 00:40:57.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:57.890 --> 00:41:00.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

